          <tr><td>Nesting Box</td><td>storage</td><td>n/a</td><td>Three or more expansions</td><td>$69 to $75</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Start with the base <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan</a>. It was $55 on Amazon the week I checked, against a $65 list price, and it seats 1 to 5. That is the version every rule in this article refers to. Full stats live on my <a href="/game/wingspan">Wingspan page</a>.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Four players is the sweet spot and five is a slog. With five, round 4 turns into a long counting exercise while the table waits for everyone to tally eggs. Two players is fast, but the card tray empties before your next turn.</p>

      <p>The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+European+Expansion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">European Expansion</a> is the first one I would add, around $30. It brings 81 new bird cards, five bonus cards, five goal tiles, and a batch of powers that trigger at the end of each round, which gives the last round something to do besides lay eggs. It also pushed my group to talk to each other more, since several of the new birds reward watching other mats.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Round-end powers slow the table down for the first few games. Everyone re-reads the same card twice before scoring, and rounds get noticeably longer until the group learns the batch.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+Oceania+Expansion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Oceania</a>, usually $32 to $35, is the one heavy players tell you to buy first, and they have a case. It adds nectar, a sixth food that works as a wild, plus five new player mats and a new set of dice. Nectar turns the early rounds loose in a way the base game never does, and the new mats make the forest row worth building past two birds. Oceania also rebalances the board on purpose: the forest and the wetland get stronger while the grassland egg engine gets weaker, and none of its new round goals reward eggs. If your table is tired of every game ending in an egg rush, this is the fix.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Nectar is generous, and some tables find that it flattens the first two rounds. It also went out of stock twice while I was writing this, so check the price rather than assuming the $32 I saw.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+Asia+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan Asia</a> is the smart buy for two players, and it is the most interesting box in the line. It has 90 new bird cards, 14 bonus cards, and two double-sided boards, and it works three ways: as a stand-alone two-player game with a whole duet map and its own goal tiles, as a plain card expansion for the base box, and as Flock mode for 6 or 7 players. It ran $44.99 on Amazon and $39 direct from Stonemaier when I checked.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Duet mode is a second ruleset, not a variation. It teaches in 15 minutes, but do not hand it to a table that has never played the base game. Flock mode also needs the base game's wooden pieces, so it is not a stand-alone 7-player game.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+Americas+Expansion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Americas</a> is the newest expansion, out since January 2026, and it is the one I bought on sight. It adds 111 bird cards plus 40 smaller hummingbird cards, a garden board, player mat overlays, and a hummingbird track that gives you a reason to play the little cards. About $30, and it carries a 4.9 star average on Amazon from over 100 ratings, which is about as much agreement as this hobby ever produces.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Those 40 hummingbird cards are mini cards, smaller than the standard bird cards, so standard sleeves will not fit them and they sit loose in the tray without the included insert. My player mat overlays also slid around until I stopped moving my mat mid-round.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+Pocket+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan Pocket</a> is not an expansion, and its birds do not mix with the base game. It is a stand-alone small box with 106 double-sided cards, powers printed as icons instead of sentences, and 25 to 35 minute games. It listed at $20 and I have seen it at $16 from online retailers. This is the box that goes in a bag. Stats are on my <a href="/game/wingspan-pocket">Wingspan Pocket page</a>.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. The icons are only obvious after you have played the base game once. Teach Pocket second, not first, or the table spends the whole game in the rulebook.</p>

      <p>Skip the Nesting Box until you own three expansions. It costs $69 to $75 and it is a storage solution, not a game.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>Here is the Shelf Test. Would this game survive 10 plays on a shelf that already holds 40 boxes? Wingspan passes that test more easily than anything else I own, and it passes for a specific reason: the rules stop being the game after night one. Ten plays in, you are not asking what a slot does. You are asking which round you should stop building, and that question never gets old.</p>

      <p>Oceania earns the space next to it for the nectar and the sharper solo game. Asia earns it if your table is two people. The rest are luxuries.</p>

      <p class="cta">Here is the part that matters. Get the base <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan</a> first, around $55, and play your first game knowing that the last two rounds are the game. Under $50 is a good price and it does hit that on sale. If your game nights are two people, put the base box back and get <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Wingspan+Asia+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Wingspan Asia</a> instead, then add the base later if you want the flock mode for 6 or 7 players. If you are still choosing a first modern board game, my <a href="/blog/best-board-games-for-beginners">beginner list</a> starts where this article ends, and the full <a href="/games/strategy">strategy lineup</a> covers what to buy after Wingspan stops surprising you. Trying to decide between it and Everdell? I put both boxes side by side in my <a href="/blog/wingspan-vs-everdell">Wingspan vs Everdell</a> breakdown.</p>

